      "note": [
        "Mortality risks in the highest ages, 95 and older, are based on a smoothing model. This is the same smoothing model that is used in the population statistics.",
        "The table is abridged with a summary for each age above 95, 95+. In this age interval the mortality risk is 1 000 per mille.",
        "From 2025 onwards, life table deaths, time in age, as well as time in age and above are no longer presented.",
        "From the reference year 2025 onwards, a small controlled random uncertainty is introduced in reported data on mean population, number of deaths and number of deaths after the birthday. The uncertainty has been added to protect individuals so that no one risks being exposed in the statistics. As a result, the reported totals are not always equal to the sum of their reported components.  \r\n  \r\nIf reported statistical values are added together to create a new total, the uncertainty is also added. There is therefore a risk that the new total contains greater uncertainty than that which – under the method – would have been added to a single value.  \r\n  \r\nThe method used for disclosure control is called Cell Key Method (CKM). You can read more about the method here: [Protection methods for disclosure control] (https://www.scb.se/en/documentation/statististical-methods/protection-methods-for-disclosure-control/) and here: [Development of disclosure protection] (https://www.scb.se/en/documentation/statistical-areas-under-change/development-of-disclosure-protection/).",
        "Correction 2026-08-31: Information for year 2025 on mean population and mortality rate, as well as on mortality risks for ages 95 and older, was revised."
      ],
